  <h2 class="project-section-title">Challenges</h2>
  <p>
    The objective is to estimate the contact state, which includes the contact locations on distinct links ($\mathbf{r}_i \in \mathbb{R}^3$), the
    corresponding 3D force vectors ($\mathbf{F}_i \in \mathbb{R}^3$), and the number of contacts (up to two), using proprioceptive sensors. This is an
    inverse problem characterized by:
  </p>
  <ul class="project-list">
    <li>
      <strong>Multi-modality (singularity):</strong> while the forward mapping (from contact to sensor reading) is deterministic, the inverse mapping
      is ambiguous; distinct contact states may correspond to identical sensor measurements, so the probability distribution of possible solutions
      becomes multi-modal.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Temporal dependency:</strong> leveraging past estimates provides a prior context for inferring the current state, for both discrete
      evolution (e.g., a new contact occurs) and continuous evolution (e.g., a contact slides).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Contact event detection:</strong> determining the number of active contacts requires identifying discrete transitions, specifically the
      addition or removal of contacts.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <div class="project-media-card is-wide">
    <a
      aria-label="Open full image: Key challenges in the contact estimation problem"
      href="{{ '/assets/img/research/contactflowfilter/fig2_challenges.webp' | relative_url }}"
      ><img
        alt="Key challenges in the contact estimation problem"
        decoding="async"
        loading="lazy"
        src="{{ '/assets/img/research/contactflowfilter/fig2_challenges.webp' | relative_url }}"
    ></a>
    <div class="card-caption">
      <strong>Examples of key challenges in the contact estimation problem.</strong> (a) Multi-modality: Two distinct contact forces ($F_{1a}$ and
      $F_{1b}$) result in identical observations. (b) Temporal dependency: Current contact states depend on history, involving discrete changes in the
      number of contacts (e.g., when a new contact $F_2$ occurs, $t-2 \rightarrow t-1$) and continuous updates in locations or forces (e.g., when an
      existing contact $F_1$ slides, $t-1 \rightarrow t$). (c) Contact event detection: Determining the number of active contacts relies on
      identifying the exact timing of a transition and whether a contact is added or removed.
    </div>
  </div>
  <h3 class="project-subsection-title">Limitations of existing approaches</h3>
  <p>
    While PF-based approaches are well-suited for addressing temporal dependency through recursive belief updates, they often fail to handle
    multi-modality due to particle starvation. In contrast, learning-based approaches excel at estimating multi-modal distributions; however, these
    feed-forward approaches are typically non-recursive and trained on temporally independent samples, thus lacking a native mechanism for leveraging
    temporal dependency. As for contact event detection, PFs rely on hard-to-tune thresholds to identify discrete transitions.
  </p>
