  <h2 class="project-section-title">Key idea: mixture of flow matching and particle filter</h2>
  <div class="project-media-card">
    <a
      aria-label="Open full image: Overview of the ContactFlowFilter framework"
      href="{{ '/assets/img/research/contactflowfilter/fig3_overview.webp' | relative_url }}"
      ><img
        alt="Overview of the ContactFlowFilter framework"
        decoding="async"
        loading="lazy"
        src="{{ '/assets/img/research/contactflowfilter/fig3_overview.webp' | relative_url }}"
    ></a>
    <div class="card-caption">
      The proposed method samples particles from two complementary proposal distributions. <em>Top:</em> The PF's temporal prediction involves
      sampling from the previous time step, random position updates, and importance weighting. <em>Bottom:</em> The flow matching samples are
      transported along a probability path ($p^{[0]} \rightarrow p^{[1]}$) to represent the contact points. <em>Right:</em> These proposals are merged
      to integrate temporal context and multi-modality, forming the combined distribution for the subsequent recursive prediction.
    </div>
  </div>
  <p>
    To formally implement the mixture of two proposal distributions, we adopt the dual-sampling concept. The output of the flow matching model serves
    as a sample from the dual-proposal distribution, $\hat{\mathbf{X}}_t \sim p_\theta(\mathbf{X}_t \mid \mathbf{O}_t) \approx \bar{q}(\mathbf{r}_t)$.
    The weight of a dual particle is computed by evaluating the Gaussian probability density of the motion model:
  </p>
  <div aria-label="Equation 2" class="project-equation" role="region" tabindex="0">
    $$ w^{(m)} \propto p\big(\mathbf{x}^{(m)}_{t,i} \mid \mathbf{x}^{(m)}_{t-1,i}\big), \qquad \mathbf{x}^{(m)}_{t-1,i} \sim \mathcal{X}_{t-1,i} $$
  </div>
  <p>
    Intuitively, dual particles that are more consistent with the previous step's particle set receive higher weights. By preserving the PF's
    recursive structure, the proposed method captures temporal dependency, while the dual-sampling step enables the direct generation of samples in
    high-belief regions, even where the particles fail to populate.
  </p>
  <h2 class="project-section-title">Technical components</h2>
  <h3 class="project-subsection-title">1. Data representation and transformer-based architecture</h3>
  <div class="project-grid project-grid-2">
    <div class="project-media-card">
      <a
        aria-label="Open full image: Observation with virtual wrench"
        href="{{ '/assets/img/research/contactflowfilter/fig10_observation.webp' | relative_url }}"
        ><img
          alt="Observation with virtual wrench"
          decoding="async"
          loading="lazy"
          src="{{ '/assets/img/research/contactflowfilter/fig10_observation.webp' | relative_url }}"
      ></a>
      <div class="card-caption">
        (a) The observation for flow matching is defined as a sequence where each link's pose $\mathbf{T}_l$ is augmented with the virtual wrench
        $\mathcal{F}_{v,l}$. (b) $\mathcal{F}_{v,l}$ is obtained by projecting the spatially distributed external base wrench and the first $l$
        elements of the external joint torque onto the corresponding frame $\{L_l\}$ via the adjoint transformation. A transformer encoder processes
        this sequence to extract cross-modal correlations.
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="project-media-card">
      <a
        aria-label="Open full image: Training sample for flow matching"
        href="{{ '/assets/img/research/contactflowfilter/fig9_sample.png' | relative_url }}"
        ><img
          alt="Training sample for flow matching"
          decoding="async"
          loading="lazy"
          src="{{ '/assets/img/research/contactflowfilter/fig9_sample.png' | relative_url }}"
      ></a>
      <div class="card-caption">
        The training sample $\mathbf{X}^{[1]} \in \mathbb{R}^{N_s \times 6}$ is constructed based on the number of contact points $n_c$: each row is
        $(\mathbf{r}_1, \mathbf{r}_1)$ for $n_c = 1$ and $(\mathbf{r}_1, \mathbf{r}_2)$ for $n_c = 2$. This paired-point representation captures
        $n_c$, thereby eliminating the need for a separate classifier.
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <h3 class="project-subsection-title">2. Flow matching with few-step inference</h3>
  <div class="project-media-card">
    <a aria-label="Open full image: Flow Matching vs DDIM" href="{{ '/assets/img/research/contactflowfilter/fig13_fm_vs_ddim.jpg' | relative_url }}"
      ><img
        alt="Flow Matching vs DDIM"
        decoding="async"
        loading="lazy"
        src="{{ '/assets/img/research/contactflowfilter/fig13_fm_vs_ddim.jpg' | relative_url }}"
    ></a>
    <div class="card-caption">
      Comparison of generative processes implemented with our architecture, using (a) Flow Matching and (b) DDIM. (•-1): Sample trajectories over ODE
      time steps (128-step integration). (•-2): Accelerated generation paths with 2-step integration. (•-3): Resulting samples via the 2-step
      integration. The sample trajectories generated by Flow Matching are markedly more linear than the curved paths produced by DDIM, which preserves
      high consistency between the results of 2-step and 128-step ODE integration.
    </div>
  </div>
  <p>
    2-step Flow Matching achieves M-RMSE values of 0.17 cm (single-contact) and 0.72 cm (dual-contact), competitively matching or even surpassing
    methods utilizing 16 ODE steps, while being eight times faster (2.57 ms). The flow matching inference is executed asynchronously on a dedicated
    thread, separate from the main algorithm.
  </p>
  <h3 class="project-subsection-title">3. Motion model with pre-computed geodesic distances</h3>
  <div class="project-media-card">
    <a aria-label="Open full image: Proposed motion model" href="{{ '/assets/img/research/contactflowfilter/fig5_motion_model.webp' | relative_url }}"
      ><img
        alt="Proposed motion model"
        decoding="async"
        loading="lazy"
        src="{{ '/assets/img/research/contactflowfilter/fig5_motion_model.webp' | relative_url }}"
    ></a>
    <div class="card-caption">
      The proposed motion model consists of two steps: (a) For the given particle, the geodesic distances to all other faces are retrieved from a
      pre-computed lookup table. (b) Weights are assigned based on the geodesic distances, and the particle for the current time step is sampled from
      the resulting distribution. This approximates Gaussian sampling directly on the robot surface, requiring approximately 0.6 µs to update a single
      particle.
    </div>
  </div>
  <h3 class="project-subsection-title">4. Flow-guided particle set management</h3>
  <p>
    For robust contact event detection, the conventional QP-based criterion is augmented by integrating generative estimates from the dual-proposal
    distribution. <strong>Increasing condition:</strong> satisfied only when the QP condition is met and the dual particles are widely spread, i.e.,
    when their standard deviation within a set exceeds that of the motion model. <strong>Decreasing condition:</strong> a particle set is a candidate
    for removal if it is not assigned any dual particles during sample allocation, and it is removed if the QP-based condition supports this removal.
  </p>
